        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_64.lnote.oe.2-2" n="2" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">353</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">illarum <hi rend="italic">HDi;</hi> <hi rend="spaced">uillarum</hi> <hi rend="italic">LbS</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">illarum HDi; <hi rend="italic">uillarum</hi> LbS</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_64.lnote.oe.3-4" n="3" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">353</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">mur. <hi rend="italic">S;</hi> murdritor <hi rend="italic">Cb</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">mur. S; murdritor Cb</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_64.lnote.oe.4-4" n="4" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">353</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">ins. <hi rend="italic">HDiLbS;</hi> trad. <hi rend="italic">Cb</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">ins. HDiLbS; trad. Cb</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_64.lnote.oe.5-4" n="5" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">353</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">sui <hi rend="italic">fügt zu LbS</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">LbS add sui</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_64.lnote.de.1" n="1" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">210</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Benutzt durch Hn 12, 1. <hi rend="italic">Vgl. Gl</hi> bussles 5c</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Used by Hn 12, 1. <hi rend="italic">Cf. Gl</hi> bussles 5c</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_64.lnote.de.2" n="2" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">210</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">S. Gl</hi> Einbruch 7a; schwerer als <hi rend="italic">hamsocn</hi> (<hi rend="italic">o</hi>. 62) beurteilt und Gebäudezerstörung mitmeinend, laut Parallele mit Langobard. Rechte; Brunner II 652f.¹¹·²³.—Tollers Übs. ‘burglary’ passt nicht, da hierunter jetzt ‘nächtlich’ mitverstanden wird</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">See Gl</hi> Einbruch 7a; judged more severe than <hi rend="italic">hamsocn</hi> (<hi rend="italic">above</hi> 62) and including destruction of the building, according to the parallel in Lombard law; Brunner II 652f.¹¹·²³.—Toller's translation ‘burglary’ does not fit, since ‘by night’ is now understood as part of that term</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_64.lnote.de.3" n="3" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">210</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Die Liste bussloser Verbrechen vermehrt dann Hn 12.—Diesen Ansatz zur Systematik schreibt Dänischem Rechte zu Steenstrup <hi rend="italic">Danelag</hi> 262</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Hn 12 then enlarges the list of unemendable crimes.—Steenstrup <hi rend="italic">Danelag</hi> 262 ascribes this beginning of systematization to Danish law</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_64.lnote.de.4" n="4" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">211</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">Vgl. Gl</hi> Brandstiftung 2ff.</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">Cf. Gl</hi> Brandstiftung 2ff.</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_64.lnote.de.5" n="5" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">211</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Diese Zeile benutzt <hi rend="italic">Hom. n</hi>. Wulfstan 274. Handhafter Diebstahl (<hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi>) und Herrenverrat kosten das Leben auch nach <hi rend="italic">o</hi>. 26. 57¹; <hi rend="italic">u</hi>. 77</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">This line is used by <hi rend="italic">Hom. n</hi>. Wulfstan 274. Theft in the act (<hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi>) and treason against one's lord cost life also according to <hi rend="italic">above</hi> 26. 57¹; <hi rend="italic">below</hi> 77</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_64.lnote.de.6" n="6" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">211</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">C's Definition ist wertvoll</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">C's definition is valuable</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_64.lnote.de.7" n="7" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">211</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Diese Zeile benutzt <hi rend="italic">Hom. n</hi>. Wulfstan 274. Handhafter Diebstahl (<hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi>) und Herrenverrat kosten das Leben auch nach <hi rend="italic">o</hi>. 26. 57¹; <hi rend="italic">u</hi>. 77</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">This line is used by <hi rend="italic">Hom. n</hi>. Wulfstan 274. Theft in the act (<hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi>) and treason against one's lord cost life also according to <hi rend="italic">above</hi> 26. 57¹; <hi rend="italic">below</hi> 77</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_64.lnote.de.8" n="8" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">211</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Begnadigung blieb dennoch möglich; <hi rend="italic">vgl. morðwyrhtan</hi>, die als <hi rend="italic">friðbenan</hi> dem König nahen, VI Atr 36, während von Hn 71, 1a = 92, 15 <hi rend="italic">misericordia</hi> für solch Verbrechen nur ironisch gemeint scheint</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Pardon nevertheless remained possible; <hi rend="italic">cf. morðwyrhtan</hi>, who approach the king as <hi rend="italic">friðbenan</hi>, VI Atr 36, whereas in Hn 71, 1a = 92, 15 <hi rend="italic">misericordia</hi> for such crime seems to be meant only ironically</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="IICn_64.robnote.n.1" n="1" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">358</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">This list is probably due to Scandinavian influence, see Steenstrup, <hi rend="italic">Danelag</hi>, p. 262 f.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="IICn_64.robnote.n.2" n="2" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">358</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">The difference between <hi rend="italic">hamsocn</hi> and <hi rend="italic">husbryce</hi> seems to be that the latter involves destruction or damage to the house itself, see Brunner, <hi rend="smallcaps">ii</hi>. p. 653²³.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="IICn_64.robnote.n.3" n="3" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">358</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Cf. II As. 6, 2, and see P. and M. <hi rend="smallcaps">ii</hi>. p. 492 f; <hi rend="italic">Pleas of Gloucester</hi>, ed. Maitland, n. 216.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="IICn_64.robnote.n.4" n="4" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">358</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Cf. Af. 4, 2; III Edg. 7, 3; II Cn. 26; 57; 77.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="IICn_64.robnote.n.5" n="5" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">358</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Cf. III Atr. 1; VIII, 1, 1; I Cn. 2, 3.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="translationFootnote" xml:id="IICn_64.whinote.n.4" n="4" source="#whitelock"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">471</biblScope></bibl><label>House-breaking</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">It is clear from the penalty that this is a different and a graver offence than <hi rend="italic">hamsocn,</hi> presumably including the destruction of buildings.</seg></note>
      </div>
